gh (n,1). Does it pass through any other points of T_n? It might pass through some other points, but we only need to cover all points. For any point with a ≤ n-1, it's covered by vertical line x=a. The only point with a=n is (n,1). So all points covered. The line is not horizontal (slope 0), not vertical (slope infinite), not -1 (since slope = -1/(n-1) which is not -1 for n≥3). So it's sunny. So k=1 works.\n\nCheck k=3: The solution defines three sunny lines L1, L2, L3 with slopes -1/2, -2, 1 respectively. They claim they cover the six points (n,1), (n-1,1), (n-2,1), (n-1,2), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). Let's verify.\n\nDefine coordinates: Points in T_n: For column x=n, only point (n,1). For column x=n-1, points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). For column x=n-2, points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). So indeed there are 1+2+3 = 6 points in the three rightmost columns.\n\nNow check L1: y-1 = -1/2*(x-n). For x=n, y-1 = 0 => y=1 => (n,1) is on L1. For x=n-2, y-1 = -1/2*(n-2-n) = -1/2*(-2) = 1 => y=2 => (n-2,2) is on L1. So L1 covers (n,1) and (n-2,2). Good.\n\nL2: y-1 = -2*(x-(n-1)). For x=n-1, y-1 = 0 => y=1 => (n-1,1) is on L2. For x=n-2, y-1 = -2*(n-2-(n-1)) = -2*(-1) = 2 => y=3 => (n-2,3) is on L2. So L2 covers (n-1,1) and (n-2,3). Good.\n\nL3: y-1 = (x-(n-2)). For x=n-2, y-1 = 0 => y=1 => (n-2,1) is on L3. For x=n-1, y-1 = (n-1-(n-2)) = 1 => y=2 => (n-1,2) is on L3. So L3 covers (n-2,1) and (n-1,2). Good.\n\nThus the three sunny lines cover all six points in the rightmost three columns. The remaining columns x=1,...,n-3 have points (c,b) with b ≤ n+1-c. For each such column, we can use vertical line x=c to cover all points in that column. There are n-3 such columns, so total lines = (n-3) vertical + 3 sunny = n lines. All lines distinct. Sunny lines are not horizontal, vertical, or slope -1: slopes are -1/2, -2, 1. Wait slope 1 is not allowed? Sunny lines are not parallel to x+y=0, which has slope -1. So slope 1 is allowed. So L3 slope = 1 is sunny. So k=3 works.\n\nThus k=0,1,3 are possible.\n\nNow we need to check if any other k are possible.\n\nWe need to see if k=2 is possible. The solution claims it's impossible. Let's examine.\n\nWe need to see if we can cover all points with exactly 2 sunny lines and n-2 non-sunny lines.\n\nWe need to see if any construction exists. Let's try to think.\n\nWe have n lines total. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Horizontal lines are y = constant. Slope -1 lines are of form x+y = constant.\n\nWe need to cover all points in T_n.\n\nObservation: Each column x=c has h_c = n+1-c points. For c = n, h_n = 1; c = n-1, h_{n-1}=2; c=n-2, h_{n-2}=3; etc.\n\nSunny lines can intersect each column at most once (since they are not vertical). So each column can have at most k points covered by sunny lines (since there are k sunny lines). The rest must be covered by non-sunny lines.\n\nNon-sunny lines: vertical lines can cover entire column; horizontal lines can cover at most one point per column (since they have constant y, but each column has points with varying y). Actually a horizontal line y = b will intersect column x=c at point (c,b) if b ≤ n+1-c. So a horizontal line can intersect each column at most once (if the point exists). Similarly, slope -1 lines x+y = s intersect each column at at most one point (since for each column x=c, y = s-c). So only vertical lines can cover multiple points in a column.\n\nThus to cover a column with h_c > k, we need at least one vertical line among the non-sunny lines. Because horizontal or slope -1 lines can only cover at most one point per column, and sunny lines also at most one per column. So if h_c > k, we need a vertical line covering that column.\n\nThus for each column with h_c > k, we need a vertical line.\n\nNow h_c = n+1-c. So columns with c ≤ n+1 - (k+1) have h_c ≥ k+1 > k. That is, columns with c ≤ n - k have h_c ≥ k+1. Let's compute: h_c > k => n+1-c > k => c n+1 - k => c ≤ n - k (since c integer). So columns 1 through n-k have h_c > k. So we need vertical lines for each of those columns.\n\nThus we need at least n-k vertical lines.\n\nBut we have total n lines, of which k are sunny, so n-k are non-sunny. So we need n-k vertical lines among the non-sunny lines. That means all non-sunny lines must be vertical. So any non-sunny line must be vertical.\n\nThus the only possible non-sunny lines are vertical lines covering columns 1 through n-k.\n\nNow we have exactly n-k vertical lines covering columns 1,...,n-k. The remaining columns are c = n-k+1,...,n. These columns have h_c ≤ k. Let's compute h_{n-k+1} = n+1-(n-k+1) = k. So column n-k+1 has exactly k points. Column n-k+2 has k-1 points, etc., down to column n has 1 point.\n\nThus the remaining columns have at most k points each, and we have exactly k sunny lines to cover them. Since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, we need to assign the k sunny lines to cover all points in those columns.\n\nNow we need to see if it's possible to cover all points in columns n-k+1,...,n using exactly k sunny lines, each intersecting each column at most once.\n\nWe have total points in those columns: sum_{i=1}^{k} i = k(k+1)/2 (since column n-k+1 has k points, column n-k+2 has k-1, ..., column n has 1). So total points = k(k+1)/2.\n\nWe have k sunny lines, each can intersect each column at most once, but can intersect multiple columns. So each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover up to (k) points (one per column) across the k columns. So total capacity of k sunny lines to cover points in those columns is at most k * (number of columns) = k * k = k^2 points. Since total points = k(k+1)/2, we need k^2 >= k(k+1)/2 => 2k^2 >= k(k+1) => 2k >= k+1 => k >= 1. So for k >= 1, capacity is enough. So capacity is not a problem.\n\nBut we need to see if we can assign points to lines such that each line is not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or slope -1. So we need to find k lines that pass through all points in those columns, each line can pass through at most one point per column.\n\nThis is reminiscent of a problem of covering a triangular grid with k lines of distinct slopes (not vertical/horizontal/-1). For k=2, we need to cover columns n-1 and n (since n-k+1 = n-1) and maybe column n-2? Let's compute for k=2: n-k = n-2, so vertical lines cover columns 1,...,n-2. The remaining columns are n-1 and n. Column n-1 has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n has point (n,1). So we have three points to cover with exactly 2 sunny lines. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most once. So we need to cover three points with two lines. Is that possible? Let's see.\n\nWe need two lines that are not vertical/horizontal/-1. They can be any other slopes. They must cover (n,1) and (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Let's try to see if we can find two lines covering these three points.\n\nOne line could be vertical? No, sunny lines cannot be vertical. So we need non-vertical lines.\n\nOption: Use line through (n,1) and (n-1,2). Slope = (2-1)/(n-1-n) = 1/(-1) = -1. That's slope -1, which is not allowed (sunny lines cannot be parallel to x+y=0). So that line is not sunny.\n\nLine through (n,1) and (n-1,1) is horizontal (slope 0), not allowed.\n\nLine through (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) is vertical, not allowed.\n\nThus any line covering two of these three points will be either horizontal, vertical, or slope -1. Let's check all pairs:\n\n- (n,1) & (n-1,1): horizontal (slope 0) -> not sunny.\n- (n,1) & (n-1,2): slope -1 -> not sunny.\n- (n-1,1) & (n-1,2): vertical -> not sunny.\n\nThus any line covering any two of these points is not sunny. So we cannot cover all three points with two sunny lines. So k=2 is impossible.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k=2 is impossible is correct.\n\nNow check k≥4. The solution claims that k≥4 is impossible because each sunny line can meet each column at most once, and we need at least one vertical line for each column with h_c > k. Let's analyze more generally.\n\nWe derived earlier that for any k, we need vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-k. So we need n-k vertical lines. Since we have n-k non-sunny lines, they must all be vertical. So we need exactly n-k vertical lines covering columns 1,...,n-k. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to cover columns n-k+1,...,n with k sunny lines. The columns are of sizes k, k-1, ..., 1. So total points = k(k+1)/2.\n\nWe have k sunny lines, each can intersect each column at most once. So each sunny line can cover at most k points (one per column). So total capacity = k^2. Since k(k+1)/2 ≤ k^2 for k≥1, capacity is enough. So capacity is not a problem for any k≥1.\n\nThus the argument that k≥4 is impossible due to insufficient vertical lines may be flawed. Let's examine the solution's argument: \"Since a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\"\n\nWe need to examine this reasoning. The solution earlier argued that for k≤2, column n-2 (size 3) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines, so one vertical line must be used for column n-2. But for k≥4, they claim that column n-2 still forces a vertical line. However, for k≥4, we have more sunny lines, maybe we can cover column n-2 with sunny lines. Let's check.\n\nIf k≥4, we have at least 4 sunny lines. Column n-2 has 3 points. Since each sunny line can intersect column n-2 at most once, we could cover all three points of column n-2 using three distinct sunny lines. So we wouldn't need a vertical line for column n-2. So the claim that column n-2 forces a vertical line for any k≥4 is false. However, the solution's argument might be that we need vertical lines for columns with h_c > k. For k≥4, h_{n-2}=3 ≤ k, so column n-2 does not require a vertical line. So the solution's argument that \"the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\" is only valid for k≤2, not for k≥4. So the argument for k≥4 is flawed.\n\nBut does that mean k≥4 is actually possible? Let's try to construct a configuration for k=4. For n≥? Let's try n=5 maybe. n=5, T_5 points: columns: x=1:5 points, x=2:4, x=3:3, x=4:2, x=5:1. We need 5 lines total, exactly k=4 sunny lines, 1 non-sunny line. Non-sunny line must be vertical (since we need to cover column 1 which has 5 points > k=4). So we need vertical line x=1 covering column 1. Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover columns 2-5. Column 2 has 4 points, column 3 has 3, column 4 has 2, column 5 has 1. So total points to cover = 4+3+2+1 = 10. We have 4 sunny lines, each can intersect each column at most once, so each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So total capacity = 4*4 = 16 > 10, so capacity is enough. But we need to find 4 lines that cover all points in columns 2-5. Is that possible? Let's try to find a construction.\n\nWe need to cover points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4); (3,1),(3,2),(3,3); (4,1),(4,2); (5,1). We have 4 lines. Each line can intersect each column at most once. So each line can be thought of as a selection of at most one point per column. So we need to assign each point to one of the 4 lines, such that each line gets at most one point per column.\n\nThis is like a problem of edge coloring a bipartite graph? Actually we can think of columns as left side, points as rows? Let's think: For each column c, we have h_c points. We need to assign each point to one of k lines, such that for each column c, each line gets at most one point. So we need to partition the points in each column into at most k groups (lines). Since each column has at most k points (for k≥4, column 2 has 4 points, which equals k). So we can assign each point in column 2 to a distinct line. Similarly for column 3 (3 points) assign to any 3 of the 4 lines, leaving one line unused for that column. So we can assign points arbitrarily as long as each line gets at most one per column.\n\nThus we can assign points to lines arbitrarily. The challenge is to ensure that each line's assigned points are collinear (i.e., lie on a single line). So we need to find 4 lines that each pass through a set of points, one per column, such that each point is assigned to exactly one line.\n\nThus the problem reduces to constructing a set of k lines that pass through the points in a triangular grid, with each column's points partitioned among the lines.\n\nThis is reminiscent of constructing a Latin rectangle or something. For k=4, we need to find 4 lines that cover all points in columns 2-5. Let's try to find a pattern.\n\nOne approach: Use lines of slopes that are rational numbers that pass through points in a systematic way. For example, we could use lines that go through (c, c') for some pattern.\n\nAlternatively, we could try to use the \"dual\" approach: Represent each point (a,b) as a line in the dual plane, and then find a set of points (lines in original) that intersect all these lines. But that's too heavy.\n\nBetter: Let's try to find a construction for general n and k=4. The solution claims it's impossible, but we suspect maybe it's possible.\n\nLet's try small n values to see if k=4 works.\n\nCase n=3: T_3 points: columns: x=1:3 points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3); x=2:2 points (2,1),(2,2); x=3:1 point (3,1). Need 3 lines total, k=4 is impossible because k cannot exceed n (k ≤ n). So k=4 not possible for n=3. So we need n≥4 for k=4.\n\nCase n=4: T_4 points: columns: x=1:4 points (1,1)-(1,4); x=2:3 points (2,1)-(2,3); x=3:2 points (3,1),(3,2); x=4:1 point (4,1). Need 4 lines total, k=4 sunny lines, 0 non-sunny lines. So all lines must be sunny. But we have 4 lines to cover all points. Since we have no vertical lines, each column must be covered by sunny lines only. Column 1 has 4 points, but we have only 4 sunny lines, each can intersect column 1 at most once, so we can assign each point in column 1 to a distinct sunny line. That's okay. Column 2 has 3 points, we need to assign them to 3 of the 4 lines, each line at most one point per column. That's okay. Column 3 has 2 points, assign to 2 lines. Column 4 has 1 point, assign to 1 line. So assignment possible. The question is can we find 4 lines that pass through the assigned points? Let's try to assign points to lines in a systematic way.\n\nWe need to assign each point (c,b) to a line L_i such that for each line L_i, the set of assigned points across columns are collinear.\n\nWe can think of constructing lines that go through points (c, f_i(c)) where f_i(c) is a function of column c (like a linear function). Since each line is linear, the points assigned to a line must satisfy a linear relationship: y = m_i x + b_i (or any line not vertical). So for each line i, we need to choose a slope m_i and intercept b_i such that for each column c where we assign a point to line i, the point (c, y) satisfies y = m_i c + b_i.\n\nThus we need to find k lines (with slopes not 0, infinite, -1) that pass through points in the triangular grid.\n\nOne approach: Use lines of the form y = a_i x + b_i where a_i is rational and b_i chosen to hit integer points. For each column c, we need to assign a point (c, y) that lies on one of the lines. So we need to choose lines such that for each column c, the set of y-values {a_i c + b_i} (for i=1..k) includes all integers from 1 to n+1-c (the points in that column). That is, for each column c, the set of values {a_i c + b_i} (i=1..k) must contain each integer in [1, n+1-c] exactly once (or at least once). Since we have k lines, we need to cover h_c points, which is ≤ k for columns c ≥ n-k+1. For columns c ≤ n-k, we have vertical lines covering them, so we don't need to worry about those columns. So we only need to cover columns c = n-k+1,...,n. For those columns, h_c = n+1-c ≤ k. So we need to assign each integer y in [1, h_c] to a distinct line i (maybe some lines not used for that column). So we need to find k lines such that for each column c in that range, the set of y-values of those lines at x=c includes the set {1,...,h_c}.\n\nThus we need to find k lines L_i: y = m_i x + b_i (with m_i not 0, not -1) such that for each c = n-k+1,...,n, the values y_i(c) = m_i c + b_i (rounded? Actually must be integer) produce exactly the set of integers from 1 to h_c (maybe in any order). Since we have k lines, we can assign each integer to a distinct line. So we need to find a set of k linear functions that produce integer values at those columns.\n\nThis is reminiscent of constructing a Latin rectangle: we need a k×k matrix of integers where each row corresponds to a line (i) and each column corresponds to c (n-k+1,...,n). The entry at (i,c) is y_i(c) = m_i c + b_i, which must be integer and must be distinct across i for each column (since each line can only cover one point per column). Also for each column c, the set of entries must be exactly {1,...,h_c} plus maybe some extra values for lines not used (if k > h_c). For columns where h_c k, some lines will produce values outside the range [1, h_c] (i.e., they will not correspond to a point in T_n). Those lines simply don't cover any point in that column.\n\nThus we need to find k lines such that for each column c, the values y_i(c) are distinct integers, and for each column c, the values that lie in the range [1, h_c] are exactly the points we need to cover.\n\nThus we need to find a set of k linear functions that produce a \"complete set\" of integers for each column.\n\nOne simple approach: Use lines of the form y = c_i (constant) horizontal lines. But horizontal lines are not allowed (sunny lines cannot be horizontal). So we cannot use horizontal lines.\n\nAnother approach: Use lines of the form y = x + b_i (slope 1). Slope 1 is allowed (sunny). For column c, y = c + b_i. So the values are c + b_i. For each column c, we need to cover values from 1 to h_c = n+1-c. So we need to choose b_i such that the set {c + b_i} (i=1..k) includes the numbers 1,...,n+1-c. That is, we need b_i = y - c for each y in [1, n+1-c]. So b_i must be of the form y - c. Since c varies, the set of b_i needed changes. So we cannot have a fixed set of b_i that works for all columns.\n\nAlternatively, use lines of slope -2, -1/2, etc. The solution for k=3 used slopes -1/2, -2, 1. They covered the rightmost three columns. For k=4, maybe we can find four lines with slopes that produce the needed points.\n\nLet's try to construct for n=5, k=4. Columns to cover: c=2,3,4,5. h_2=4, h_3=3, h_4=2, h_5=1. So we need to cover points:\n\nc=2: y=1,2,3,4\nc=3: y=1,2,3\nc=4: y=1,2\nc=5: y=1\n\nWe need 4 lines L1,...,L4. For each column, each line can cover at most one point. So we need to assign each point to a line.\n\nOne possible assignment: assign each line to a \"diagonal\" pattern: L1 covers (2,1), (3,2), (4,3?), but (4,3) is not in T_5 because h_4=2, so (4,3) is not allowed. So we need to be careful.\n\nBetter: Let's try to assign lines as follows:\n\n- L1: (2,1), (3,2), (4,3?), not allowed. So maybe L1: (2,1), (3,2), (5,1)? That would be points (2,1), (3,2), (5,1). Are they collinear? Let's check slope between (2,1) and (3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1. So line slope 1 passes through (2,1) and (3,2). Does it also pass through (5,1)? For slope 1, line equation y - 1 = 1*(x-2) => y = x - 1. At x=5, y=4, not 1. So (5,1) not on that line. So L1 cannot cover those three points.\n\nAlternatively, we could try to use lines of slope 0? Not allowed. Slope -1? Not allowed. So we need slopes other than 0, infinite, -1.\n\nWe can try to find lines that pass through (2,4) and (5,1). Slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So can't use that.\n\nTry line through (2,4) and (3,3): slope = (3-4)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n\nTry line through (2,4) and (4,2): slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nTry line through (2,4) and (5,1): slope -1, not allowed.\n\nThus any line connecting a point in column 2 with a point in column 5 will have slope (y5 - y2)/(5-2). Since y5 = 1, y2 ∈ {1,2,3,4}. So slopes are (1-1)/3 = 0 (horizontal) for y2=1, (1-2)/3 = -1/3 (allowed), (1-3)/3 = -2/3 (allowed), (1-4)/3 = -1 (not allowed). So we can have slopes -1/3 and -2/3, which are allowed (not -1, not 0). So we could try to use lines with slopes -1/3 and -2/3.\n\nLet's try to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points. This is a combinatorial design problem. Let's try to systematically search for a solution for n=5, k=4.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each line can be described by slope m_i and intercept b_i (or any two points). Since we have only 4 lines, we can try to assign each point to a line and then see if the assigned points are collinear.\n\nWe need to assign points in column 2 (4 points) to 4 distinct lines (since each line can cover at most one point per column). So each line must cover exactly one point in column 2. So we can assign each line L_i to a distinct point (2, y_i) where y_i ∈ {1,2,3,4}. So we have a bijection between lines and points in column 2.\n\nNow for column 3 (3 points), we need to assign each point (3, y) to a line, but we have 4 lines, so one line will not be used for column 3. So we need to assign each of the three points to three distinct lines among the four.\n\nSimilarly for column 4 (2 points) assign to two distinct lines, and column 5 (1 point) assign to one line.\n\nThus each line will have a set of assigned points: one from column 2, maybe one from column 3, maybe one from column 4, maybe one from column 5. The line must pass through all its assigned points.\n\nThus each line will have at most 4 points (one per column). Since we have 4 columns, each line could have up to 4 points.\n\nThus we need to find a set of 4 lines such that each line passes through its assigned points.\n\nWe can think of constructing a Latin rectangle: we need to assign each line a \"code\" that determines which point in each column it covers.\n\nOne approach: Use lines of slope m_i = (y_i - 1)/(c_i - 5) maybe? Let's try to find a systematic pattern.\n\nWe can try to use lines that pass through (2, y_i) and (5,1). For each y_i ∈ {1,2,3,4}, the line connecting (2, y_i) to (5,1) has slope (1 - y_i)/(5-2) = (1 - y_i)/3. For y_i=1, slope = 0 (horizontal) not allowed. So we cannot assign line to (2,1) and (5,1) because that would be horizontal. So the line covering (5,1) cannot also cover (2,1). So the line covering (5,1) must be assigned to a point (2, y) with y ≠ 1. So possible y_i = 2,3,4. For y_i=2, slope = (1-2)/3 = -1/3 (allowed). For y_i=3, slope = -2/3 (allowed). For y_i=4, slope = -1 (not allowed). So line covering (5,1) can be assigned to (2,2) or (2,3). Let's pick (2,2) for line L1: line through (2,2) and (5,1) slope -1/3, equation: y - 2 = -1/3 (x - 2) => y = -1/3 x + 8/3? Let's compute: y = -1/3 x + (2 + 2/3) = -1/3 x + 8/3. At x=3, y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.666..., not integer. So line L1 does not pass through any integer point at x=3 or x=4. So L1 would only cover points (2,2) and (5,1). That's okay; it doesn't need to cover points in columns 3 and 4.\n\nNow we need to cover points in column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). We have 3 remaining lines (L2, L3, L4). They each have a point in column 2 assigned (the remaining points (2,1),(2,3),(2,4)). Let's assign:\n\n- L2: (2,1)\n- L3: (2,3)\n- L4: (2,4)\n\nNow we need to assign points in column 3 to these lines. Since we have 3 points, we can assign each to a distinct line. Let's try to assign:\n\n- L2: (3,1)\n- L3: (3,2)\n- L4: (3,3)\n\nNow we need to check if each line L2, L3, L4 can pass through its assigned points (2,y2) and (3,y3). Let's compute slopes:\n\n- L2: (2,1) to (3,1): slope = (1-1)/(3-2) = 0 (horizontal) not allowed. So L2 cannot have (3,1). So we need to assign differently.\n\nTry assign L2: (3,2) maybe? (2,1) to (3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1 (allowed). So L2 can be line y = x - 1 (since passes through (2,1): y = x - 1). At x=3, y=2, matches. At x=4, y=3 (but (4,3) not in T_5). At x=5, y=4 (not needed). So L2 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\nNow L3: (2,3) assigned. We need to assign a point in column 3 to L3. Options: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). Let's try (3,1): slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2 (allowed). So line L3: passes through (2,3) and (3,1). Equation: slope -2, y - 3 = -2 (x - 2) => y = -2x + 7. At x=4, y = -8 + 7 = -1 (not in T_5). At x=5, y = -10 + 7 = -3. So L3 covers only those two points. That's fine.\n\nNow L4: (2,4) assigned. Need to assign a point in column 3 to L4. Options: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). Let's try (3,3): slope = (3-4)/(3-2) = -1 (not allowed). So cannot assign (3,3). Try (3,2): slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2 (allowed). So line L4: passes through (2,4) and (3,2). Equation: slope -2, y - 4 = -2 (x - 2) => y = -2x + 8. At x=4, y = -8 + 8 = 0 (not in T_5). At x=5, y = -10 + 8 = -2. So L4 covers (2,4) and (3,2). But note (3,2) is already covered by L2? Actually L2 covers (3,2). So we cannot assign (3,2) to both L2 and L4. So we need distinct points per column. So we need to assign each point in column 3 to a distinct line. So far we have L2 covering (3,2). L3 covering (3,1). L4 cannot cover (3,2) because it's already used. L4 could cover (3,3) but slope -1 not allowed. So L4 cannot cover any remaining point in column 3. So this assignment fails.\n\nLet's try a different assignment for column 2 points to lines.\n\nWe need to assign points in column 2 to lines such that each line can also cover a point in column 3 with allowed slope.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each must have a distinct point in column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). Let's denote lines L1-L4.\n\nWe need to assign points in column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) to three of the lines, leaving one line not covering column 3.\n\nWe need to ensure each line's slope between its column 2 point and column 3 point is not 0, -1, infinite.\n\nLet's consider possible slopes between (2,y2) and (3,y3). Slope = (y3 - y2)/(3-2) = y3 - y2. So slope = y3 - y2. So slope must not be 0, -1. So we need y3 - y2 ≠ 0, -1. So y3 ≠ y2 and y3 ≠ y2 - 1.\n\nThus for each line, we need to pick y3 such that y3 ≠ y2 and y3 ≠ y2 - 1.\n\nNow we need to assign distinct y3 values for each line used.\n\nLet's list possible pairs (y2, y3) where y2 ∈ {1,2,3,4}, y3 ∈ {1,2,3} (since column 3 has only up to 3). And we need y3 ≠ y2, y3 ≠ y2 - 1.\n\nCompute possibilities:\n\n- y2=1: y3 cannot be 1 (y3=y2) and cannot be 0 (y2-1=0). So allowed y3 ∈ {2,3}. So (1,2) slope=1, allowed; (1,3) slope=2, allowed.\n\n- y2=2: y3 cannot be 2 (y3=y2) and cannot be 1 (y2-1=1). So allowed y3 ∈ {3}. So (2,3) slope=1, allowed.\n\n- y2=3: y3 cannot be 3 (y3=y2) and cannot be 2 (y2-1=2). So allowed y3 ∈ {}? Actually column 3 only has y up to 3, so possible y3 values are {1,2,3}. Excluding 3 and 2 leaves only y3=1. So (3,1) slope = -2, allowed.\n\n- y2=4: y3 cannot be 4 (not in column 3) and cannot be 3 (y2-1=3). So allowed y3 ∈ {1,2}. So (4,1) slope = -3, allowed; (4,2) slope = -2, allowed.\n\nThus possible assignments for each line:\n\n- L with (2,1) can go to (3,2) or (3,3).\n- L with (2,2) can go to (3,3) only.\n- L with (2,3) can go to (3,1) only.\n- L with (2,4) can go to (3,1) or (3,2).\n\nNow we need to assign three distinct lines to cover the three points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). Let's try to assign:\n\n- (3,1) could be covered by line from (2,3) or (2,4).\n- (3,2) could be covered by line from (2,1) or (2,4).\n- (3,3) could be covered by line from (2,1) or (2,2).\n\nWe need to assign each column 2 point to a distinct line, and each column 3 point to a distinct line.\n\nOne possible assignment:\n\n- L1: (2,1) -> (3,2) (allowed)\n- L2: (2,2) -> (3,3) (allowed)\n- L3: (2,3) -> (3,1) (allowed)\n- L4: (2,4) -> (no column 3 point) (unused for column 3)\n\nThus we have lines:\n\n- L1: passes through (2,1) and (3,2). Slope = 1, line y = x - 1.\n- L2: passes through (2,2) and (3,3). Slope = 1, line y = x? Actually (2,2) and (3,3) slope = (3-2)/(3-2) = 1, line y = x? Check: y = x? At (2,2) yes, at (3,3) yes. So L2: y = x.\n- L3: passes through (2,3) and (3,1). Slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, line y = -2x + 7 (since at x=2, y=3 => 3 = -4 + b => b=7). So L3: y = -2x + 7.\n- L4: (2,4) only (so far). It may also cover points in columns 4 and 5.\n\nNow we need to cover column 4 points: (4,1),(4,2). We have 4 lines; each line can cover at most one point in column 4. L1 currently covers (2,1) and (3,2). It can also cover a point in column 4 if it lies on the line y = x - 1. At x=4, y = 3. That's not a point in column 4 (which has y=1,2). So L1 cannot cover any point in column 4.\n\nL2: line y = x. At x=4, y=4, not in column 4 (y must be ≤2). So L2 cannot cover column 4.\n\nL3: line y = -2x + 7. At x=4, y = -8 + 7 = -1, not in column 4. So L3 cannot cover column 4.\n\nL4: currently only has (2,4). We can choose its slope to pass through a point in column 4. Let's try to assign L4 to cover (4,2) maybe. The line through (2,4) and (4,2) has slope (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, which is not allowed (slope -1). So cannot use (4,2). Try (4,1): slope = (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed (not -1). So line through (2,4) and (4,1) slope = -3/2. Equation: y - 4 = -3/2 (x - 2) => y = -3/2 x + 7. So L4: y = -1.5 x + 7. At x=5, y = -7.5 + 7 = -0.5, not integer. So L4 covers (2,4) and (4,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover column 5 point (5,1). Which line can cover (5,1)? Let's see if any of L1-L4 passes through (5,1).\n\n- L1: y = x - 1 => at x=5, y=4, not 1.\n- L2: y = x => at x=5, y=5, not 1.\n- L3: y = -2x + 7 => at x=5, y = -10 + 7 = -3, not 1.\n- L4: y = -1.5 x + 7 => at x=5, y = -7.5 + 7 = -0.5, not 1.\n\nThus none cover (5,1). So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nWe could try to assign L4 to cover (5,1) instead of (4,1). Let's see if we can assign L4 to cover (5,1) and maybe also (4,2) or (4,1). Let's try line through (2,4) and (5,1): slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So cannot use (5,1) with (2,4). So L4 cannot cover (5,1) if it also covers (2,4). So maybe we need to assign a different point in column 2 to L4, not (2,4). But we already assigned (2,4) to L4 because we needed each line to have a distinct column 2 point. However, we could reassign column 2 points differently. Let's try a different assignment for column 2 points to lines.\n\nWe need to assign each line a distinct column 2 point. We can choose which line gets which point. The assignment we used gave L4 (2,4). Maybe we can assign L4 to (2,2) or (2,1) etc. But we need to keep the assignments for column 3 points consistent.\n\nLet's try a different assignment: maybe we can assign L4 to (2,2) and L2 to (2,1) etc. Let's systematically search for a solution.\n\nWe have 4 lines L1-L4. Each line must have a distinct point in column 2. Let's denote the column 2 point for line i as (2, a_i) where a_i ∈ {1,2,3,4} distinct.\n\nWe also need to assign column 3 points (3,b_i) to some lines (maybe not all). For each line i that gets a column 3 point, we need b_i ∈ {1,2,3} distinct across lines that get a column 3 point. Also slope m_i = (b_i - a_i) must not be 0 or -1.\n\nSimilarly for column 4 points (4,c_i) where c_i ∈ {1,2} distinct across lines that get a column 4 point, slope from column 2 to column 4 must be (c_i - a_i)/(4-2) = (c_i - a_i)/2, which must not be 0, -1, infinite. Also slope from column 3 to column 4 must be consistent if line has both column 3 and column 4 points.\n\nSimilarly for column 5 point (5,1) must be covered by some line.\n\nWe need to find a set of lines that satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe can treat each line as defined by two points (maybe more). Since we have 4 lines, we can try to assign each line to cover exactly one point in each column (except maybe some columns have fewer points than lines). For column 5, only one line will cover (5,1). For column 4, two lines will cover (4,1) and (4,2). For column 3, three lines will cover (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). For column 2, all four lines will cover (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). So each line will have exactly one point in each column, except that some lines will have points in columns where there are fewer points than lines; those lines will have no point in that column.\n\nThus we can think of a 4x4 assignment matrix where rows are lines, columns are column indices (2,3,4,5). For each column c, we need to assign each point (c,y) to a distinct line. So we need a bijection between points in column c and a subset of lines of size h_c. For column 2, h_2=4, so each line gets a point. For column 3, h_3=3, so three lines get a point, one line gets none. For column 4, h_4=2, two lines get a point, two lines get none. For column 5, h_5=1, one line gets a point, three lines get none.\n\nThus each line will have a set of points across columns, possibly missing some columns.\n\nNow we need to ensure that for each line, the points assigned to it are collinear (i.e., lie on a line with slope not 0, -1, infinite). Since each line can have up to 4 points (if it gets a point in each column), but we can also have lines with fewer points.\n\nWe need to find a configuration of assignments and slopes that works.\n\nThis is a combinatorial design problem. Let's try to find a solution manually.\n\nWe can try to assign points to lines such that each line's points lie on a line with slope not 0, -1, infinite.\n\nOne approach: Use lines of slope 1 (allowed). For slope 1, points satisfy y - x = constant. So each line of slope 1 corresponds to a constant d = y - x. For column c, the point (c, y) on that line must satisfy y = c + d. So for each column c, the line will have a point if y = c + d is within the allowed range [1, h_c]. So we need to choose d such that for each column c, c + d is an integer between 1 and h_c inclusive.\n\nSimilarly, we can use lines of slope -2, -1/2, etc.\n\nLet's explore using slope 1 lines. For n=5, columns c=2,3,4,5. For each line with slope 1, we have y = x + d. For column 2, y = 2 + d must be in {1,2,3,4}. So d ∈ {-1,0,1,2}. For column 3, y = 3 + d must be in {1,2,3}. So d ∈ {-2,-1,0}. For column 4, y = 4 + d must be in {1,2}. So d ∈ {-3,-2}. For column 5, y = 5 + d must be in {1}. So d = -4.\n\nThus the only possible d that works for all columns is d = -4? Let's check: d = -4 gives y = x - 4. For column 2, y = -2 (not allowed). So slope 1 lines cannot cover all columns simultaneously. But we can have lines that cover only some columns.\n\nWe need to assign each line a constant d_i such that for each column c where the line is assigned a point, the point (c, c + d_i) is within the allowed range. So we can have lines with different d_i.\n\nLet's list possible d values for each column:\n\n- Column 2: allowed d ∈ {-1,0,1,2} (since y = 2 + d ∈ {1,2,3,4} => d ∈ {-1,0,1,2}).\n- Column 3: allowed d ∈ {-2,-1,0} (since y = 3 + d ∈ {1,2,3} => d ∈ {-2,-1,0}).\n- Column 4: allowed d ∈ {-3,-2} (since y = 4 + d ∈ {1,2} => d ∈ {-3,-2}).\n- Column 5: allowed d = -4 (since y = 5 + d = 1 => d = -4).\n\nThus we need to assign each line a d_i such that for each column c where the line gets a point, d_i is in the allowed set for that column.\n\nWe have 4 lines. Let's try to assign d_i values to lines:\n\n- One line must cover column 5 point (5,1). So that line must have d = -4. So we have a line L5 with d = -4 (slope 1). This line will have points (c, c-4) for any column c where c-4 is within allowed range. Let's see which columns c have c-4 ∈ allowed range:\n\nc=2: y = -2 (not allowed)\nc=3: y = -1 (not allowed)\nc=4: y = 0 (not allowed)\nc=5: y = 1 (allowed)\n\nThus L5 only covers column 5 point. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to cover column 4 points (4,1) and (4,2). We need two lines that cover these points. Let's see possible d values for column 4: d = -3 gives y = 1; d = -2 gives y = 2. So we need lines with d = -3 and d = -2.\n\nThus we can have L4a with d = -3 (covers (4,1)), and L4b with d = -2 (covers (4,2)). These lines may also cover points in other columns if allowed.\n\nCheck L4a (d=-3): For column 2, y = -1 (not allowed). For column 3, y = 0 (not allowed). So L4a only covers column 4 point (4,1). Similarly L4b (d=-2): For column 2, y = 0 (not allowed). For column 3, y = 1 (allowed). So L4b can also cover column 3 point (3,1). Indeed (3,1) is allowed. So L4b could cover (3,1) and (4,2). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover column 3 points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). We already have L4b covering (3,1). So we need to cover (3,2) and (3,3) with other lines.\n\nPossible d values for column 3: d = -2 gives y=1 (already used by L4b), d = -1 gives y=2, d = 0 gives y=3. So we need lines with d = -1 (covers (3,2)) and d = 0 (covers (3,3)). Let's consider lines L3a (d=-1) and L3b (d=0).\n\nCheck L3a (d=-1): For column 2, y = 1 (allowed). So L3a can also cover (2,1). For column 4, y = 3 (not allowed). For column 5, y = 4 (not allowed). So L3a can cover (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\nCheck L3b (d=0): For column 2, y = 2 (allowed). For column 4, y = 4 (not allowed). For column 5, y = 5 (not allowed). So L3b can cover (2,2) and (3,3). Good.\n\nNow we have covered column 2 points (2,1) and (2,2) via L3a and L3b. We still need to cover (2,3) and (2,4). We have lines L4a (d=-3) and L5 (d=-4) left. L4a (d=-3) cannot cover column 2 (y=-1). L5 (d=-4) cannot cover column 2 (y=-2). So we need to assign (2,3) and (2,4) to some lines. But we have no lines left that can cover column 2 points (2,3) and (2,4). So this assignment fails.\n\nThus slope 1 lines alone cannot cover all points.\n\nWe can consider using lines of other slopes.\n\nAlternatively, we can try to use lines of slope -2 (or other slopes) to cover multiple columns.\n\nLet's try to find a systematic approach: For n=5, k=4, we need to cover columns 2-5 with 4 lines. This is a small instance; we could try to brute force search for a solution. But we need to reason manually.\n\nWe can try to find a solution using lines of slopes -2, -1/2, 2, etc.\n\nLet's try to assign lines as follows:\n\n- L1: line through (2,1) and (5,1) is horizontal (slope 0) not allowed. So cannot use that.\n\n- L1: line through (2,1) and (4,2) slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 (allowed). Equation: y - 1 = 0.5 (x - 2) => y = 0.5 x. At x=3, y = 1.5 (not integer). So L1 would not cover any point in column 3. That's okay; it can skip column 3. At x=5, y = 2.5 (not integer). So L1 would only cover (2,1) and (4,2). That's okay.\n\n- L2: line through (2,2) and (5,1) slope = (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3 (allowed). Equation: y - 2 = -1/3 (x - 2) => y = -1/3 x + 8/3. At x=3, y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3 (not integer). At x=4, y = -4/3 + 8/3 = 4/3 (not integer). So L2 only covers (2,2) and (5,1). That's okay.\n\n- L3: line through (2,3) and (3,1) slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2 (allowed). Equation: y = -2x + 7. At x=4, y = -8 + 7 = -1 (not in T_5). At x=5, y = -10 + 7 = -3. So L3 covers (2,3) and (3,1). Good.\n\n- L4: line through (2,4) and (3,2) slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2 (allowed). Equation: y = -2x + 8. At x=4, y = -8 + 8 = 0 (not in T_5). At x=5, y = -10 + 8 = -2. So L4 covers (2,4) and (3,2). So far we have covered points:\n\nColumn 2: (2,1) L1, (2,2) L2, (2,3) L3, (2,4) L4 (all covered).\nColumn 3: (3,1) L3, (3,2) L4, (3,3) not covered yet.\nColumn 4: (4,1) not covered, (4,2) L1.\nColumn 5: (5,1) L2.\n\nWe still need to cover (3,3), (4,1). We have no lines left (we used all 4 lines). So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nWe could try to have some lines cover more points, e.g., a line that covers (3,3) and (4,1) maybe.\n\nLet's see if there is a line that passes through (3,3) and (4,1). Slope = (1-3)/(4-3) = -2 (allowed). Equation: y - 3 = -2 (x - 3) => y = -2x + 9. At x=2, y = -4 + 9 = 5 (not in column 2). At x=5, y = -10 + 9 = -1 (not in column 5). So this line could cover (3,3) and (4,1). So we could assign a line L5 (but we only have 4 lines). So we need to incorporate this line into one of the existing lines, perhaps replace some assignment.\n\nLet's try to reassign lines to cover all points.\n\nGoal: Use 4 lines to cover all points in columns 2-5. Let's denote lines L1-L4.\n\nWe need to cover 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. Each line can