int per column as well? Actually a line x+y=c intersects each column at exactly one point (if any). So slope -1 lines also intersect each column at most one point. So vertical lines are the only non-sunny lines that can cover multiple points in a column. Horizontal lines can cover multiple points in a row, but rows have decreasing lengths as well.\n\nThus to maximize coverage, we would use vertical lines for as many columns as possible, because they can cover many points per column. The non-sunny lines that are not vertical (horizontal or slope -1) can cover at most one point per column, similar to sunny lines. So the total number of points covered by non-vertical non-sunny lines is at most (n-k) (if all non-sunny lines are non-vertical) times n? Actually each non-vertical line can cover at most one point per column, so at most n points per line. So total coverage by all lines (including sunny) is at most (k + (n-k)) * n = n^2 points. But we only need to cover n(n+1)/2 points, which is less than n^2 for n≥1. So that bound is not restrictive.\n\nBut we need to consider that vertical lines are limited to n columns, and we have only n lines total. If we have many sunny lines, we might not have enough vertical lines to cover the columns with many points. The columns with many points are the leftmost columns. For each column a, we need to cover n+1-a points. If we don't have a vertical line for column a, we need to cover those points using other lines, each of which can cover at most one point in that column. So if we have no vertical line for column a, we need at least n+1-a lines (sunny or non-vertical non-sunny) to cover all points in that column. So the total number of lines needed is at least sum over columns without vertical lines of (n+1-a). Since we have only n lines total, we need to ensure that the sum of required lines for columns without vertical lines plus the number of vertical lines used does not exceed n.\n\nThus we can try to prove that if k≥4, we cannot