o n) has points (i, b) where b ranges from 1 to n+1-i. So column i has n+1-i points. So column 1 has n points, column 2 has n-1 points, ..., column n has 1 point. So the number of points per column decreases linearly.\n\nWe have n lines total. Non-sunny lines can be vertical (cover entire column), horizontal (cover entire row), or slope -1 (cover diagonal lines of constant x+y). Sunny lines can be any other slope.\n\nGoal: cover all points with n lines, exactly k sunny.\n\nWe need to find possible k.\n\nWe can think of covering each column with a line. Since we have n columns and n lines, a natural approach is to assign one line per column. If we use vertical lines for each column, we have k=0. That's a valid construction.\n\nIf we want k=1, we can replace one vertical line with a sunny line that covers the points in that column (or some points) while still covering all points. For example, we can keep vertical lines for columns 1 to n-1, and use a sunny line to cover column n (which has only one point). That's possible.\n\nIf we want k=3, we can replace three vertical lines with three sunny lines that cover the points in those columns (or maybe cover multiple columns). The solution suggests using three sunny lines to cover the 6 rightmost points (columns n-2, n-1, n). Let's examine: columns n-2 has 3 points, n-1 has 2 points, n has 1 point. That's total 6 points. They claim three sunny lines can cover those 6 points. Indeed, we can find three lines each covering two points (or one covering three). For n=3 we gave an example. For general n, we need to show that we can cover the rightmost three columns with three sunny lines. That seems plausible.\n\nNow, can we have k=2? That would require covering the rightmost three columns (or some other set) with two sunny lines and the rest with vertical lines. Let's see if it's possible.\n\nWe have columns n-2 (3 points), n-1 (2 points), n (1 point). That's 6 points. Two lines can cover at most 2 points each if they are not ve